WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.803
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Emma. Welcome to Online Newspaper Class, level three.

2
00:00:06.103 --> 00:00:09.687
Hi! I'm Visal. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.987 --> 00:00:17.016
Unit fourteen: Youth Political Engagement. Today you will express wishes and regrets with wish and if only.

4
00:00:17.316 --> 00:00:19.957
Here is our lesson map. Let's start!

5
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:24.168
Part one: words. You will learn eight new words.

6
00:00:25.017 --> 00:00:26.122
Youth vote.

7
00:00:27.117 --> 00:00:30.486
Youth vote means the votes cast by young people in an election.

8
00:00:31.083 --> 00:00:33.303
The youth vote decided the close result.

9
00:00:33.917 --> 00:00:36.441
Your turn. Say: Youth vote.

10
00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:40.173
Youth vote.

11
00:00:40.917 --> 00:00:42.289
Political apathy.

12
00:00:43.283 --> 00:00:46.606
Political apathy means a lack of interest in politics.

13
00:00:47.217 --> 00:00:50.505
Political apathy is common among first-time voters.

14
00:00:51.117 --> 00:00:53.711
Your turn. Say: Political apathy.

15
00:00:56.567 --> 00:00:57.916
Political apathy.

16
00:00:58.650 --> 00:01:00.127
Student activism.

17
00:01:01.117 --> 00:01:05.194
Student activism means political action organised by students.

18
00:01:05.783 --> 00:01:09.164
Student activism brought climate issues to the debate.

19
00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.971
Your turn. Say: Student activism.

20
00:01:15.833 --> 00:01:17.136
Student activism.

21
00:01:17.883 --> 00:01:18.966
Voting age.

22
00:01:19.950 --> 00:01:23.459
Voting age means the minimum age at which a person may vote.

23
00:01:24.050 --> 00:01:26.793
Some countries have lowered the voting age to sixteen.

24
00:01:27.383 --> 00:01:29.850
Your turn. Say: Voting age.

25
00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:33.887
Voting age.

26
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:36.081
Civic education.

27
00:01:37.083 --> 00:01:41.521
Civic education means teaching about rights, duties and how government works.

28
00:01:42.117 --> 00:01:44.801
Civic education explains how laws are made.

29
00:01:45.417 --> 00:01:48.173
Your turn. Say: Civic education.

30
00:01:51.033 --> 00:01:52.592
Civic education.

31
00:01:53.350 --> 00:01:54.502
Youth-led movement.

32
00:01:55.517 --> 00:01:59.873
Youth-led movement means a campaign organised mainly by young people.

33
00:02:00.483 --> 00:02:02.878
A youth-led movement demanded cleaner air.

34
00:02:03.483 --> 00:02:06.542
Your turn. Say: Youth-led movement.

35
00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:10.958
Youth-led movement.

36
00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:13.240
Political awareness.

37
00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:18.711
Political awareness means knowing what is happening in politics and why it matters.

38
00:02:19.317 --> 00:02:22.593
Political awareness grows when news is easy to understand.

39
00:02:23.183 --> 00:02:26.579
Your turn. Say: Political awareness.

40
00:02:29.433 --> 00:02:30.980
Political awareness.

41
00:02:31.717 --> 00:02:33.031
First-time voter.

42
00:02:34.017 --> 00:02:37.247
First-time voter means a person who votes for the first time.

43
00:02:37.850 --> 00:02:41.080
Every first-time voter received a guide to registration.

44
00:02:41.683 --> 00:02:44.719
Your turn. Say: First-time voter.

45
00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:48.974
First-time voter.

46
00:02:49.767 --> 00:02:53.290
Part two: dialogue. Let's listen to a conversation.

47
00:02:54.150 --> 00:02:59.449
Visal, a young voter says 'If only more of my friends had voted.' What does 'if only' express?

48
00:03:00.550 --> 00:03:13.851
It expresses a strong wish or regret. For the past we use if only / I wish + past perfect: I wish I had registered. For the present, past simple: I wish the voting age were lower.

49
00:03:14.950 --> 00:03:16.299
And for annoying behaviour?

50
00:03:17.383 --> 00:03:26.386
We use would: I wish politicians would listen to the youth vote. We cannot use would with the same subject: not 'I wish I would vote'.

51
00:03:27.483 --> 00:03:29.111
Can I use it in an article?

52
00:03:30.217 --> 00:03:42.111
Yes, to express regret or hope: If only civic education had started earlier, political apathy might be lower. It adds emotion, so use it carefully.

53
00:03:43.267 --> 00:03:44.802
Part three: grammar.

54
00:03:45.650 --> 00:03:50.239
Grammar focus: Wishes and regrets: I wish and If only.

55
00:03:50.539 --> 00:03:51.760
Look at the pattern.

56
00:03:52.450 --> 00:03:55.367
If only more students had voted in the last election.

57
00:03:56.283 --> 00:03:59.026
Many young people wish the voting age were lower.

58
00:03:59.917 --> 00:04:03.356
First-time voters wish politicians would listen to them.

59
00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:15.177
Past regret: wish / if only + past perfect. Present wish: wish + past simple (were for all subjects). Annoyance about others: wish + would. Never I wish I would.

60
00:04:15.917 --> 00:04:19.985
Be careful! Don't say: I wish I would vote last year.

61
00:04:21.933 --> 00:04:24.571
Say: I wish I had voted last year.

62
00:04:24.871 --> 00:04:27.765
Use wish + past perfect for a past regret.

63
00:04:28.567 --> 00:04:30.929
Part four: reading. Read and listen.

64
00:04:31.733 --> 00:04:33.210
Where Are the Young Voters?.

65
00:04:33.510 --> 00:04:38.099
Young people care about politics, yet their youth vote is often the lowest.

66
00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:43.549
Many first-time voters say they wish they had learned more through civic education.

67
00:04:44.533 --> 00:04:49.435
If only schools had taught how elections work, political apathy might have been smaller.

68
00:04:50.433 --> 00:04:57.123
Student activism and youth-led movements show that awareness is high, and some campaigners wish the voting age were lower.

69
00:04:58.133 --> 00:05:01.631
What they need most is trust that taking part will change something.

70
00:05:02.733 --> 00:05:06.422
Part five: quiz. Five questions. Are you ready?

71
00:05:07.283 --> 00:05:09.654
If only I blank registered earlier!

72
00:05:09.954 --> 00:05:13.190
A, have. B, had. C, would have.

73
00:05:16.733 --> 00:05:18.489
The answer is B: had.

74
00:05:19.650 --> 00:05:21.719
I wish the voting age blank lower.

75
00:05:22.019 --> 00:05:25.243
A, were. B, would be. C, had been.

76
00:05:28.800 --> 00:05:30.393
The answer is A: were.

77
00:05:31.550 --> 00:05:34.757
A lack of interest in politics is political blank.

78
00:05:35.057 --> 00:05:38.861
A, awareness. B, education. C, apathy.

79
00:05:42.400 --> 00:05:44.841
The answer is C: apathy.

80
00:05:45.983 --> 00:05:47.704
Choose the correct sentence.

81
00:05:48.004 --> 00:05:55.268
A, I wish I would vote last year.. B, I wish I had voted last year.. C, I wish I voted last year..

82
00:05:58.833 --> 00:06:02.052
The answer is B: I wish I had voted last year..

83
00:06:03.217 --> 00:06:07.515
According to the reading, young people need trust that taking part will change…

84
00:06:07.815 --> 00:06:11.341
A, nothing. B, something. C, everything.

85
00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:16.783
The answer is B: something.

86
00:06:18.000 --> 00:06:21.581
Part six: speaking. It's your turn to talk.

87
00:06:22.417 --> 00:06:26.309
What could encourage more young people to vote and stay politically engaged?

88
00:06:35.117 --> 00:06:36.582
Now listen to Visal.

89
00:06:36.882 --> 00:07:04.617
More young people would vote if politics felt relevant to their lives. Schools can teach civic education with real examples, and governments can make registration simple, even online. Candidates should talk about issues such as jobs, housing and climate in language young people understand. When youth see that their votes lead to visible change, they are more likely to keep participating.

90
00:07:04.917 --> 00:07:06.708
Excellent answer, Visal!

91
00:07:07.617 --> 00:07:13.764
Great work! Now you can use eight new words and wishes and regrets: i wish and if only.

92
00:07:14.064 --> 00:07:19.154
Today's value is public engagement. Share knowledge so that everyone can learn from it.

93
00:07:19.454 --> 00:07:22.106
See you in Unit fifteen. Goodbye!

94
00:07:22.406 --> 00:07:25.709
Thank you, Ms. Emma! Bye, everyone!

